# The `caos-conversation` tool (SPEC, "Tools"): the std/go worker image with
# this directory's script curried onto it, plus the `help` the agent registers
# it by. Like caos-test-result it reads BY HASH and touches no source tree, so
# it needs no `@in`.
#
# The docs are a HERE-STRING, so they live in the `.caos-expr` — which is
# STRIPPED from the tree the expression is evaluated against. Editing them
# re-keys this tool's arg tree and nothing else: no build sees these bytes.
HELP=<<END
Read a recorded conversation — for example a Claude Code session that `caos mcp hook`
recorded — given the hash of the commit at its tip (`caos head <hash>`, or the `C` a
conversation ref points to). Prints it in order: each user message, each assistant
message, and each tool call with its arguments and result. Arguments and results are
cut to `width` characters; pass `call` to see one call in full. Use it to review how a
session went and to find what the docs or tools should have said.

WHAT IS NOT IN THE RECORD. The hooks record only the user's prompts, the calls to caos
tools with their results, and each turn's final assistant message. The model's own
text and reasoning between tool calls are not recorded, nor are calls to tools that
are not caos's, nor calls that never reached one (a misnamed tool, a denied call).
Commit timestamps are fixed, so there are no timings. A gap in the story usually
means an unrecorded step, not a missing one. The known gaps are listed in this tool's
recording-gaps.md.
@param hash The full 40-character hash of the conversation's tip commit.
@param [call] Print only this tool call (its `toolu_...` id, as this tool prints it), untruncated.
@param [width] Cut each tool call's arguments and result to this many characters (default 300; 0 for no cut).
@param [msg_width] Cut each user and assistant message to this many characters (default 0: no cut). `width` does not touch messages, which are most of a long conversation's output.
@param [from] First transcript entry to print, by the number shown as `[n]` (default 0). With `to`, pages a long conversation.
@param [to] Last transcript entry to print, inclusive (default: the end).
@param [only] `user`, `assistant`, or `failed`. `failed` prints the user messages and only the calls that did not complete or returned an error, to find where a session stumbled.

The header lists calls by tool and the entries holding a failed call. A long conversation
overflows what a caller can read (the excess is saved to a file it usually cannot open), so
start with `only=failed` or `msg_width=200`, then page with `from` and `to`.
END
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/go --worker1:@=worker.go --help=$HELP
